   "body_html": "If anyone votes down this question then please mention your comment, it is very tough for me, hopefully not for others.... :)"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 17423998,
   "parent_id": 17423967,
   "author_user_id": 440558,
   "author_display_name": "Some programmer dude",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/17423998",
   "score": 13,
   "created_utc": "2013-07-02T11:09:17Z",
   "last_edit_utc": "2013-07-02T11:35:25Z",
   "last_activity_utc": "2013-07-02T11:35:25Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>It's because the size of <code>char</code> is one, and the size of <code>int</code> is four. This means that adding <code>4</code> to <code>ptr</code> makes the result point to the second entry in the <code>int</code> array.</p>\n\n<p>If you compiled this on a big <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Endianness\" rel=\"noreferrer\">endian</a> system you would have printed 33554432 instead.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 17424019,
   "parent_id": 17423967,
   "author_user_id": 1126268,
   "author_display_name": "md5",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/17424019",
   "score": 3,
   "created_utc": "2013-07-02T11:10:14Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2013-07-02T11:10:14Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<pre><code>int main(){\n int arr[] = {1,2,3,4,5};\n char *ptr = (char *) arr;\n printf(\"%d\",*(ptr+4));\n return 0;\n}\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Each case of <code>arr</code> has <code>sizeof(int)</code> size (which may be 4 on your implementation). </p>\n\n<p>Since <code>ptr</code> is a pointer to <code>char</code>, <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pointer_%28computer_programming%29#C_and_C.2B.2B\" rel=\"nofollow\">pointer arithmetic</a> makes <code>ptr + 4</code> points 4 bytes after <code>&amp;arr[0]</code>, which may be <code>&amp;arr[1]</code>.</p>\n\n<p>In memory, it looks like something like:</p>\n\n<pre><code>Address | 0 1 2 3 | 4 5 6 7 | ...\nValue   |  arr[0] |  arr[1] | ...\n</code></pre>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 25306079,
   "parent_id": 17423967,
   "parent_type": "question",
   "author_user_id": 74660,
   "author_display_name": "Lucas",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/17423967/#comment25306079_17423967",
   "score": 12,
   "created_utc": "2013-07-02T11:10:28Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "You have an &quot;int&quot; array, but a &quot;char&quot; pointer."
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 25306085,
   "parent_id": 17423967,
   "parent_type": "question",
   "author_user_id": 2339452,
   "author_display_name": "Dayal rai",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/17423967/#comment25306085_17423967",
   "score": 7,
   "created_utc": "2013-07-02T11:10:43Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "+1 on your announcement :)"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 17424073,
   "parent_id": 17423967,
   "author_user_id": 2380830,
   "author_display_name": "Bathsheba",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/17424073",
   "score": 2,
   "created_utc": "2013-07-02T11:12:46Z",
   "last_edit_utc": "2013-07-02T11:20:45Z",
   "last_activity_utc": "2013-07-02T11:20:45Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>On a 32 bit platform, <code>int</code> is four times the size of <code>char</code>. When you add 4 to <code>ptr</code>, you add 4 times the <em>size of what ptr points to</em> to ptr (which itself is a memory location). That happens to be the address of the second element in the <code>int</code> array.</p>\n\n<p>On a 64 bit platform, <code>int</code> is <strong>eight</strong> times the size of <code>char</code>; and your output would be very different.</p>\n\n<p>To cut a long story short, your code is not portable, (also see Joachim Pileborg's answer re endianness) but amusing to unpick.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 17424079,
   "parent_id": 17423967,
   "author_user_id": 2496731,
   "author_display_name": "ondrejdee",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/17424079",
   "score": 2,
   "created_utc": "2013-07-02T11:13:08Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2013-07-02T11:13:08Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>What you do is definitely not recommended in production code, but is definitely great for understanding pointers, casts, etc. in the learning process, so for this your example is great. So, why you get 2. It is because your array is an array of ints, which depending on your architecture has different size (in your case, <code>sizeof(int)</code> is 4). You define <code>ptr</code> as being a char pointer, char has size 1 byte. Pointer arithmetics (that's what you do when you write <code>ptr+4</code>) works with size of objects the pointer references, in your case with chars. Thus <code>ptr+4</code> is 4 bytes away from the beginning of your array, and thus at the 2nd position of your <code>int</code> array. That is it. Try <code>ptr+5</code>, you should get 0.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 17424129,
   "parent_id": 17423967,
   "author_user_id": 1611055,
   "author_display_name": "Andreas Fester",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": true,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/17424129",
   "score": 81,
   "created_utc": "2013-07-02T11:15:21Z",
   "last_edit_utc": "2013-07-02T12:20:50Z",
   "last_activity_utc": "2013-07-02T12:20:50Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>Assumed a little endian architecture where an int is 32 bits (4 bytes), the individual bytes of <code>int arr[]</code> look like this (least significant byte at the lower address. All values in hex):</p>\n\n<pre><code>|01 00 00 00|02 00 00 00|03 00 00 00|04 00 00 00|05 00 00 00\n</code></pre>\n\n\n\n<pre><code>char *ptr = (char *) arr;\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Now, <code>ptr</code> points to the first byte - since you have casted to <code>char*</code>, it is treated as char array onwards:</p>\n\n<pre><code>|1|0|0|0|2|0|0|0|3|0|0|0|4|0|0|0|5|0|0|0\n ^\n +-- ptr\n</code></pre>\n\n\n\n<p>Then, <code>*(ptr+4)</code> accesses the fifth element of the char array and returns the corresponding <code>char</code> value:</p>\n\n<pre><code>|1|0|0|0|2|0|0|0|3|0|0|0|4|0|0|0|5|0|0|0\n         ^\n         +-- *(ptr + 4) = 2\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Hence, <code>printf()</code> prints <code>2</code>.</p>\n\n<p>On a <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Endianness\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Big Endian</a> system, the order of the bytes within each <code>int</code> is reversed, resulting in</p>\n\n<pre><code>|0|0|0|1|0|0|0|2|0|0|0|3|0|0|0|4|0|0|0|5\n         ^\n         +-- *(ptr + 4) = 0\n</code></pre>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 25306242,
   "parent_id": 17423998,
   "parent_type": "answer",
   "author_user_id": 2380830,
   "author_display_name": "Bathsheba",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/17423967/#comment25306242_17423998",
   "score": 1,
   "created_utc": "2013-07-02T11:15:37Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "I thought that endian only applied to floating point. Are you suggesting that integers behave in the same way? I&#39;m not so sure."
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 17424162,
   "parent_id": 17423967,
   "author_user_id": 1644091,
   "author_display_name": "dijkstra",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/17424162",
   "score": 1,
   "created_utc": "2013-07-02T11:16:56Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2013-07-02T11:16:56Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>Since you are coverting int* to char*, ptr[0] = 1, ptr[4] = 2, ptr[8] = 3, ptr[12] = 4 , ptr[16] = 5 and all others equal to 0. ptr+4 points to 4th element in the ptr array. So result is 2.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 25306287,
   "parent_id": 17423998,
   "parent_type": "answer",
   "author_user_id": 148423,
   "author_display_name": "Joe",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/17423967/#comment25306287_17423998",
   "score": 5,
   "created_utc": "2013-07-02T11:16:58Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "Wherever there is a type that uses more than 1 byte there is a question of what order to store the bytes in."
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 25306315,
   "parent_id": 17423998,
   "parent_type": "answer",
   "author_user_id": 440558,
