  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 17424199,
   "parent_id": 17423967,
   "author_user_id": 2432477,
   "author_display_name": "nouney",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/17424199",
   "score": 1,
   "created_utc": "2013-07-02T11:18:55Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2013-07-02T11:18:55Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<pre><code>int main(){\n int arr[] = {1,2,3,4,5};\n char *ptr = (char *) arr;\n printf(\"%d\",*(ptr+4));\n return 0;\n}\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Imagine <code>arr</code> is stored at the address <code>100</code> (totally dumb address). So you have:\n<code>arr[0]</code> is stored at the address 100.\n<code>arr[1]</code> is stored at the address 104. (there's is +4 because of the type <code>int</code>)\n<code>arr[2]</code> is stored at the address 108.\n<code>arr[3]</code> is stored at the address 112. Etc etc.</p>\n\n<p>Now you're doing <code>char *ptr = (char *) arr;</code>, so <code>ptr</code> = 100 (the same as <code>arr</code>).\nThe next statement is interesting, specially the second argument of <code>printf</code> : <code>*(ptr+4)</code>.\nKeep in my mind that <code>ptr</code> = 100. So <code>ptr + 4</code> = 104, the same address that <code>arr[1]</code> ! So it will print the value of <code>arr[1]</code>, which is 2.</p>\n"
  },
