   "score": 3,
   "created_utc": "2013-07-02T11:44:40Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "Your description of how <code>printf()</code> handles this case is incorrect. It&#39;s getting the value of the 5th character as an integer, not a pointer to same. Also, as Joachim says, your endianness is flipped."
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 25307624,
   "parent_id": 17424073,
   "parent_type": "answer",
   "author_user_id": 2048554,
   "author_display_name": "Matthieu Rouget",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/17423967/#comment25307624_17424073",
   "score": 1,
   "created_utc": "2013-07-02T11:53:59Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<code>int</code> storage size is implementation dependent. On most 64 bits platforms, <code>sizeof(int)</code> is 4. See <a href=\"http://stackoverflow.com/questions/384502/what-is-the-bit-size-of-long-on-64-bit-windows\" title=\"what is the bit size of long on 64 bit windows\">stackoverflow.com/questions/384502/&hellip;</a> for more infos."
  },
